-- chem/ringmoment.hypercubical — the moments of the electron's hop on a ring of n vertices around which the geometry
-- carries a loop of spin j, as integers. Begun from chem/ring.hypercubical and nothing else: its head states that the
-- electron's spectral measure on the ring is the ring with twist U averaged over the loop state's distribution of U,
-- and its checked law gives the coherence of w circuits, 0 at odd w and −u/(J(J+1)) at w = 2u for 1 ≤ u ≤ J = 2j
-- (`ring-law`). With the hop of unit amplitude between neighbours, the 2h-th moment at a vertex is the sum over
-- closed walks of 2h steps of the coherence of the walk's winding. A walk of 2h steps on the line ends d places
-- away in binom(2h, h + d/2) ways, and winding w on the ring of n is d = w·n, so with w = ±2u
--   J(J+1) · moment(2h) = J(J+1) · binom(2h, h) − 2 · Σ_{u=1}^{J} u · binom(2h, h + u·n).
-- The sum is `rm-loss n h J`. On the hexagon (n = 6), the smallest ring of the diamond net: the loss is 0 for
-- every 2h ≤ 10, so those moments are the line's own (the tree's), and at 2h = 12 the loss is 1, the two walks
-- that go twice round: J(J+1) · moment(12) = 924 · J(J+1) − 2. That is the first moment at which the electron on a
-- hexagon is affected by the loop, and by how much, at every spin.
-- A leaf: no module imports it.
import "ring.hypercubical"

